on answering, and named entity recognition, especially on the GLUE benchmark.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Statistical Framework for Alignment with Biased AI Feedback (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08259v1)\nciteturn31653view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view3\",\"lineno\":250}); Total lines: 458\nL237: ### 4.2 Theoretical Results for DIPO\nL238: \nL239: We begin by characterizing the DIPO estimator through an influence function expansion (cite64†van der Vaart, 2000 ), which clarifies how bias correction and variance reduction arise from combining AI-labeled and human-labeled preference data. We first consider the estimation of the preference probability $\\mathbb{P}(\\pi(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot))$ using only human-labeled data.\nL240: In this setting, to achieve statistically optimal estimation of the preference probability, we adopt a semiparametrically efficient estimator. The estimator is obtained as the solution to the estimating equation\nL241: \nL242:  | $$\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\psi(X,Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)},Z;\\pi,\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}},\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}},g)\\right]=0,$$  |\nL243: \nL244: where the influence function $\\psi(\\cdot)$ is given by\nL245:  | $$\\begin{split}&\\mathbb{E}_{Y\\sim\\pi(\\cdot\\mid X),\\,Y^{\\prime}\\sim\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot\\mid X)}\\big\\{g(Y,Y^{\\prime}\\mid X)\\big\\}\\\\\nL246: &\\;+\\frac{1}{2}\\{Z-g(Y^{(1)},Y^{(2)}\\mid X)\\}\\frac{\\pi(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)-\\pi(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y^{(1)}\\mid X)\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y^{(2)}\\mid X)}.\\end{split}$$  |  | (4.2)\nL247: The first term corresponds to a direct plug-in estimator of the preference probability under the human preference model $g$, while the second term augments this estimate with a weighted residual. This augmentation corrects for model misspecification and yields a doubly robust estimating equation: consistency holds if either $g$ or $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$ is known or correctly specified.\nL248: ###### Lemma 1.\nL249: \nL250: Suppose $n$ i.i.d. samples $\\{(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},Z_{i})\\}_{i=1}^{n}$ are observed from $\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{Human}}$. Treating $\\pi$ and $\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ as fixed, the efficient influence function for estimating $P(\\pi(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot))$ is\nL251: \nL252:  | $$\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\psi(X_{i},Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)},Z_{i};\\pi,\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}},\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}},g)-P(\\pi(\\cdot)\\succ\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(\\cdot)),$$  |\nL253: where $g$ and $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$ are nuisance parameters.\nL254: Lemma cite65†1 establishes the influence function (cite66†4.2 ) for constructing a semiparametrically efficient estimator. In contrast to cite63†Xu et al. (2025) , we treat $\\pi$ and $\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ as known and regard $g$ and $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$ as nuisance components. This formulation clearly reflects practical alignment pipelines, where the policy and reference model are fixed during preference estimation.\nL255: Moreover, the influence function in (cite66†4.2 ) naturally motivates the DIPO estimator as a debiased construction: the first term provides a model-based estimate via $g$, and the second term serves as a bias correction, removing systematic bias induced by AI evaluation. We impose the following additional assumptions.\nL256: ###### Assumption 5 (Weak coverage).\nL257: \nL258: There exists $\\epsilon>0$ such that the ratios $\\pi/\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$ and $\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}/\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$, as well as their estimates when $\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}$ is unknown, are bounded between $\\epsilon$ and $1/\\epsilon$. Moreover, the clipping thresholds satisfy $c_{\\min}\\leq\\epsilon$ and $c_{\\max}\\geq 1/\\epsilon$.\nL259: ###### Assumption 6 (Policy realizability).\nL260: \nL261: The optimal policy $\\pi^{*}$ defined in (cite67†3.5 ) is contained in the policy class $\\Pi$.\nL262: Assumption cite68†5 is a standard overlap condition ensuring that all feasible responses receive non-negligible probability under the policies of interest. Compared with Assumption cite53†2 for DDPO, this requirement is substantially weaker, highlighting the broader applicability of DIPO. Assumption cite68†5 also ensures the importance ratios already lie in $[c_{\\min},c_{\\max}]$. Thus, clipping is inactive and is used only for numerical stability.\nL263: Assumption cite69†6 ensures that the policy class is rich enough to contain the optimal solution.\nL264: ###### Theorem 2 (Asymptotic expansion of DIPO).\nL265: \nL266: Under Assumptions cite58†4 , cite68†5 , and cite69†6 , the estimator $\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi)$ defined in Algorithm cite48†2 admits the expansion\nL267:  | $$\\begin{split}&\\widehat{P}_{\\mathrm{DIPO}}(\\pi)\\\\\nL268: =&\\;\\frac{1}{2}\\sum_{a=1}^{2}\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=n+1}^{n+N}g(Y_{i},Y_{i}^{(a)}\\mid X_{i})\\\\\nL269: &\\;+\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{\\pi(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})-\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})\\pi(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}\\{Z_{i}-g(Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})\\}\\\\\nL270: &\\;-\\frac{1}{2n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{\\pi(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})-\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})\\pi(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}{\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(1)}\\mid X_{i})\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}(Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})}\\{\\widehat{Z}_{i}-\\widehat{g}(Y_{i}^{(1)},Y_{i}^{(2)}\\mid X_{i})\\}\\\\\nL271: &\\;+R,\\end{split}$$  |  | (4.3)\nL272: where, with probability at least $1-\\delta$,\nL273: \nL274:  | $$|R|\\leq C\\!\\left(\\frac{\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|}{\\sqrt{N}}+\\frac{\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|+\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|}{\\epsilon\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\\!\\sqrt{\\log(1/\\delta)}+\\frac{\\|\\widehat{g}-g\\|\\cdot\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}/\\widehat{\\pi}_{\\mathrm{Gen}}^{\\mathrm{Hum}}-1\\|}{\\epsilon^{2}}.$$  |\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNear-Oracle KV Selection via Pre-hoc Sparsity for Long-Context Inference (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08329v1)\nciteturn31653view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31650view2\",\"lineno\":100}); Total lines: 994\nL39:     3. cite29†IV-C Early Token Freezing L40:     4. cite30†IV-D Parallel Acceleration L41:       1. cite31†Sequential execution L42:       2. cite32†Parallel acceleration L43:   6. cite33†V Experiments L44:     1. cite34†V-A Experimental Setup L45:     2. cite35†V-B Results on GSM8K and COQA L46:       1. cite36†V-B1 Datasets and Metrics L53:     4. cite43†V-D Efficiency Evaluation L54:       1. cite44†V-D1 Inference Detail L55:       2. cite45†V-D2 Results Analysis L56:         1. cite46†Operator latency L57:         2. cite47†End-to-end throughput L58:         3. cite48†Effect of block size L59:     5. cite49†V-E Further Analysis L60:       1. cite50†V-E1 CIS Dilation L61:       2. cite51†V-E2 Hyperparameter Tuning L62:         1. cite52†Setting L63:         2. cite53†CIS L64:         3. cite54†PSAW L65:         4. cite55†ETF L66:         5. cite56†CPE L67:       3. cite57†V-E3 Similarity Ablations L68:   7. cite58†VI Conclusion L69:   8. cite59†VII Unified Information Bounds L70:     1. cite60†VII-A Setup L71:     2. cite61†VII-B Two universal technical lemmas L72:   9. cite62†VIII Information Bounds of Token-sparse Attention L73:     1. cite63†VIII-A Oracle top-$k$ selection L74:     2. cite64†VIII-B Post–hoc sparsity L75:       1. cite65†Token-Dropping Oracle (TDO) L76:       2. cite66†Query-Aware Approximation (QAA) L77:     3. cite67†VIII-C Pre–hoc sparsity L78:   10. cite68†References L79:   11. cite69†-D Mean-shift Property of Token-sparse Attention L80:     1. cite70†-D1 Self-Attention as Mean-Shift Clustering L81:       1. cite71†Implicit Clustering in Attention L82:       2. cite72†Mean-Shift Interpretation L83:     2. cite73†-D2 Among-Query Property L84:       1. cite74†Problem Setting L85:       2. cite75†Intuition via Mean-Shift L86:       3. cite76†Formal Derivation of the Among-Query Property L87:       4. cite77†Transmission to Token-sparse Attention L88:       5. cite78†Smooth Shift of the Attention Centroid L89:     3. cite79†-D3 Centroid Shift L90:     4. cite80†-D4 CIS MI Bound Parameterization L91:       1. cite81†Notation L92:       2. cite82†Covering drift with radius $r$ L93:       3. cite83†When $r<s(\\tau)$ (under-dilation) L94:   12. cite84†-E Exponential Recency Beyond Sink L95:   13. cite85†-F PSAW Bounds on Dropped Mass L96:   14. cite86†-G ETF Bounds on Additional Mass Gap L97:   15. cite87†-H Joint pre-hoc retained-mass certificate and tuning L98: cite88†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L99: \nL100: arXiv:2602.08329v1 [cs.LG] 09 Feb 2026\nL101: # Near-Oracle KV Selection via Pre-hoc Sparsity for Long-Context Inference Thanks: This work was supported in part by the Guangdong Major Project of Basic and Applied Basic Research (2023B0303000016), Guangdong S&T Program (No.\nL102: 2024B0101050002), Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Joint Laboratory of Human-Machine Intelligence-Synergy Systems (2019B121205007), CAS Key Technology Talent Program, Colleges and Universities Key Laboratory of Intelligent Integrated Automation (No.201502), CAS Key Laboratory of Human-Machine Intelligence-Synergy Systems, Shenzhen Institutes of Advanced Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences (2014DP173025). Thanks: Manuscript received July 1, 2025; revised XX, 2025.\nL103: (Corresponding author: jun.cheng@siat.ac.cn)Thanks: Yifei Gao is with University College London, London, UK. (E-mail: ucabaoj@ucl.ac.uk).Thanks: Lei Wang and Jun Cheng are with the Shenzhen Institutes of Advanced Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS), Shenzhen 518055, China. They are also with the Chinese University of Hong Kong.\nL104: (e-mail: lei.wang1@siat.ac.cn, jun.cheng@siat.ac.cn).Thanks: Rong-Cheng Tu, Qixin Zhang and Dacheng Tao are with the College of Computing & Data Science, Nanyang Technological University, Singapore. (E-mail: rongcheng.tu@ntu.edu.sg, qixin.zhang@ntu.edu.sg, dacheng.tao@ntu.edu.sg)\nL105: Yifei Gao$\\dagger$    Lei Wang$\\dagger$    Rong-Cheng Tu$\\dagger$    Qixin Zhang Affiliation: Jun Cheng^{*},  and Dacheng Tao,\nL106: ###### Abstract\nL107: A core bottleneck in large language model (LLM) inference is the cost of attending over the ever-growing key–value (KV) cache. Although near-oracle top-$k$ KV selection can preserve the quality of dense attention while sharply reducing computation and bandwidth, existing sparse methods generally rely on posterior heuristics—selectors conditioned on observed attention or proxy scores.\nL108: Such conditioning introduces posterior bias: it tends to distort the true token importance and miss salient tokens, thereby impairing long-range reasoning. To tackle this problem, we propose Pre-hoc Sparsity (PrHS), which selects KV entries before attention scoring and provides explicit accuracy control. Let the attention mass of discarded entries be $\\delta$ (the dropped mass).\nL109: Through a marginal-to-mutual-information analysis, we derive an upper bound on the mutual-information loss that depends only on $\\delta$. This relation both explains the failure modes of posterior heuristics and enables verifiable guarantees by controlling $\\delta$ in advance.\nL110: Within PrHS, we instantiate three orthogonal pre-hoc selectors along the axes of time, depth, and layer: (i) temporal query sharing, justified by a Lipschitz bound on attention-centroid drift so that a modest dilation of the previously shared set covers the next step; (ii) recency-window retention, leveraging distance-decay priors to guarantee retained attention mass; and (iii) cross-layer KV sharing, exploiting high inter-layer similarity to eliminate redundant retrieval and computation.\nL111: Extensive experiments on LLaMA and Mistral families validate the superiority of PrHS. On LLaMA and Mistral families across GSM8K and CoQA, the approach reduces retrieval overhead by ${>}90\\%$, achieving $3\\times$ higher retrieval sparsity than the state-of-the-art HShare at matched or better accuracy.\nL112: It also incurs ${<}1\\%$ average degradation on LongBench, lowers attention FLOPs by ${\\sim}15\\%$ versus prior sparse baselines, and yields a $9.9\\times$ speedup in attention-operator latency and $2.8\\times$ higher throughput on NVIDIA A100-80GB GPUs than the original dense baseline.\nL113: ## I Introduction\nL114: Large Language Models (LLMs) [cite89†9 , cite90†14 , cite91†41 ] empower advanced applications such as multi-turn dialogue [cite92†47 , cite93†31 , cite94†22 ], long-form document summarization [cite95†50 , cite96†20 ], and multimodal tasks [cite97†36 , cite98†18 , cite99†13 , cite100†33 , cite101†25 , cite102†35 ] involving text and visual understanding [cite103†48 , cite104†8 , cite105†37 , cite106†16 , cite107†11 , cite108†34 ].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOJBKQ: Objective-Joint Babai-Klein Quantization (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08376v1)\nciteturn31653view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31650view3\",\"lineno\":80}); Total lines: 516\nL63: In OJBKQ, Random-$K$ expands the quality of the candidate set, while JTA improves how we select the optimal candidate.\nL64: \nL65: Our contributions are summarized as follows:\nL66: \nL67:   * •\nL68: \nL69: Quantization Formulation. We revisit layer-wise PTQ from a CVP perspective and reformulate it as an explicit lattice-decoding problem. This enables a faithful Babai-style implementation that operates via triangular factors and back-substitution, without materializing any matrix inverse.\nL70: \nL71:   * •\nL72: Superior Sub-optimal Solution. We extend Klein’s randomized algorithm to the box-constrained case and apply it to PTQ. By running $K$ independent probabilistic traces in parallel, our method explores non-orthogonal lattice bases to find superior integer candidates while maintaining strong language modeling performance with low perplexity in both 4‑bit and 3‑bit configurations. In designing and implementation of our numerical algorithm, we take both accuracy and efficiency into account.\nL73: For example, unlike GPTQ, no inverse of a matrix is computed. To reduce the computational overhead of multiple indepandent solution, we design a GPU-efficient algorithm presented in Appendix cite31†A L74:   * •\nL75: \nL76: JTA Candidate Selection. We propose the Joint Target Alignment (JTA) objective, a unified scoring criterion that subsumes commonly used PTQ objectives as special cases by varying the alignment target between runtime-quantized and full-precision references, providing a more fine-grained criterion for candidate selection under error propagation.\nL77: ## 2 Related Work\nL78: ##### Layer-wise post-training quantization (PTQ).\nL79: Post-training quantization is a practical standard for compressing large language models without retraining. Following recent PTQ taxonomies (cite46†Zhao et al., 2025 ), mainstream weight-only PTQ methods can be broadly grouped by their dominant mechanism.\nL80: Compensation-based approaches, exemplified by GPTQ (cite37†Frantar et al., 2023 ), quantize weights sequentially while updating the remaining unquantized weights to compensate for accumulated quantization errors, often leveraging second-order curvature surrogates estimated from calibration data. Rotation-based methods such as QuIP (cite39†Chee et al., 2024 ) apply structured transformations to reshape weight distributions and improve quantizability under low-bit constraints.\nL81: Salience-based methods, represented by AWQ (cite38†Lin et al., 2024 ), reduce quantization error by selecting scaling factors according to activation/weight importance, avoiding mixed-precision deployment.\nL82: In addition, optimization-based approaches (e.g., OmniQuant^{1}^{1} 1 Although it is classified as PTQ in the survey, it still requires training on the calibration dataset to find scales.Then we exclude it from our baselines and PoTPTQ) (cite47†Shao et al., 2024 ; cite48†Wang et al., 2025 ) directly optimize quantization parameters with lightweight objectives while keeping pretrained weights frozen.\nL83: Overall, existing PTQ pipelines mainly differ along two orthogonal axes: (i) the approximate solver used to handle the discrete subproblem, and (ii) the objective used to evaluate candidates and guide selection.\nL84: ##### Integer least squares and Babai-type solution.\nL85: The integer least squares (ILS) problems or/and its box-constrained variant (BILS) arise in crypotograph (cite49†Micciancio and Goldwasser, 2002 ), GPS (cite50†Teunissen, 1996 ; cite51†Chang et al., 2005 ; cite52†Chang et al., 2023 ), MIMO detection (cite53†Agrell et al., 2002a ; cite54†Bai et al., 2014 ) etc, where the goal is to recover a discrete-valued vector under a quadratic objective.\nL86: Since the ILS and BILS are NP-hard (cite55†Micciancio, 2002 ; cite56†Verdú, 1989 ), in some application often efficient sub-optimal solutions are applied. An often used method is Babai’s nearest-plane algorithm (cite41†Babai, 1986 ), which computes the Babai point by back substitution on an upper-triangular linear system, where rounding is performed at each step.\nL87: The Babai point is the first integer point found by the Schnorr–Euchner sphere-decoding algorithm, which enumerates integer points in an ellipsoid to find the optimal solution (cite57†Schnorr and Euchner, 1994 ). It has been extended to the box-constrained case (cite58†Wen and Chang, 2021 ).\nL88: While computationally efficient, when the coefficient matrix (referred to as the lattice basis matrix in lattice theory) is not well-conditioned (or not well-reduced in lattice theory), or when the dimension of the integer vector is large. There are other sub-optimal algorithms, such as those proposed in (cite59†Barbero and Thompson, 2006 ; cite60†Chang et al., 2024 ), but they are not suitable for GPU computing.\nL89: Fortunately, there is a randomized version of the Babai algorithm proposed in (cite45†Klein, 2000 ), which is not widely used yet. In each step of the Babai algorithm, Klein’s algorithm rounds the real solution to a nearby integer according to a predefinded distribution. When the algorithm is called $K$ times, it generates $K$ points. Then the best one, which gives the minimum residual, is chosen as the final sub-optimal solution. This method can be parallelized.\nL90: The method is for unconstrained ILS problem and has not been widely used yet. Recently, the connection between sequential PTQ solvers and the Babai point has gained attention(cite40†Chen et al., 2025 ). This paper emphasizes that the PTQ problems can be modeled as BILS problems so that sub-optimal BILS algorithms can be applied.\nL91: ##### Objectives under error propagation and distribution drift.\nL92: A recurring challenge in layer-wise PTQ is that minimizing a layer-local reconstruction proxy does not necessarily translate to improved end-to-end quality, due to quantization error propagation and the resulting input distribution drift across layers. Consequently, the choice of the alignment target—whether to match outputs under runtime (partially-quantized) activations or to match full-precision reference outputs—plays a central role in practical PTQ performance.\n"
